  That last point is the blocker, so one question before I design.

  [1mThe fork:[0m a parameter is something the [3mcaller[0m must already have. Right now the submit handler has only what the form
  collected — there is no user ID anywhere to pass in. So "add a userId parameter" only works if we decide where that
  value comes from, and the three answers lead to different interfaces.

  - [1mClient-generated correlation ID[0m — the handler mints an ID (e.g. [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m) per attempt and passes it in,
    so logs for one attempt can be tied together. Buys traceability immediately with no backend dependency; costs you
    the fact that it identifies an [3mattempt[0m, not a person. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mUsername is already the identity[0m — no new parameter; [38;5;153mlogin()[39m logs and returns the username it was given. Buys zero
    new surface, and honestly already satisfies "track who logged in" for a stub. Costs nothing, rules out nothing.
  - [1mServer returns the ID[0m — the real API knows the account ID; it comes back in the [3mresponse[0m, not in. That's a
    return-value change ([38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m), not a parameter. Buys a real, stable identity; costs a stub that
    fakes the value until the API exists. This is the one that's expensive to reverse, because callers start depending
    on the returned shape.

  [1mMy recommendation: the client-generated correlation ID[0m, if you want the parameter you asked for. It's the only one
  where a [3mparameter[0m is the right shape, it works today with no backend, and it gives you something the username can't —
  distinguishing two attempts by the same person. But if "who logged in" means the account identity, the third option is
  what you actually want, and I'd rather say so before writing the wrong signature.

  Either way this changes [38;5;153mlogin[39m's signature, so I'll wait for your call before touching it.
